The various mapping cylinders that make up the universal cover of $K\Gamma$ are arranged in a treelike pattern. The tree in question, call it $T\Gamma$ , has one vertex for each copy of a universal cover of a $K(G_{
u},1)$ in $	ilde{K}$ , and two vertices are joined by an edge whenever the two universal covers of $K(G_{
u},1)$ 's corresponding to these vertices are connected by a line segment lifting a line segment in the mapping cylinder structure of a mapping cylinder of $K\Gamma$ . The inductive construction of $	ilde{K}$ is reflected in an inductive construction of $T\Gamma$ as a union of an increasing sequence of subtrees $T_{1}\subset T_{2}\subset\cdots$ Corresponding to $	ilde{K}_{1}$ is a subtree $T_{1}\subset T\Gamma$ consisting of a central vertex with a number of edges radiating out from it, an 'asterisk' with possibly an infinite number of edges. When we enlarge $	ilde{K}_{1}$ to $	ilde{K}_{2},$ $T_{1}$ is correspondingly enlarged to a tree $T_{2}$ by attaching a similar asterisk at the end of each outer vertex of $T_{1}$ , and each subsequent enlargement is handled in the same way. The action of $\pi_{1}(K\Gamma)$ on $	ilde{K}$ as deck transformations induces an action on $T\Gamma$ , permuting its vertices and edges, and the orbit space of $T\Gamma$ under this action is just the original graph $\Gamma$ . The action on $T\Gamma$ will not generally be a free action since the elements of a subgroup $G_{
u}\subset\pi_{1}(K\Gamma)$ fix the vertex of $T\Gamma$ corresponding to one of the universal covers of $K(G_{
u},1)$ .

